course: dictionary
lesson: 1486
title: Cause and effect
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Causation is the link between a cause and what it makes happen. The cause is why something happens, and the effect is what happens because of it. «The heavy rain made the river rise» shows a cause (the rain) and an effect (the river rising).
explain: Two things can happen at the same time without one causing the other. «Ice cream sales and sunburn both go up in summer» is a pattern that is called correlation. Hot weather is more likely to be the cause of both. Grown-ups say, «correlation is not causation», to warn you not to jump to conclusions.
explain: Sometimes the cause goes the other way round. «Big fires get more firefighters and also do more damage» does not mean the firefighters cause the damage. The fire comes first and brings the firefighters. Mixing up which way the cause goes is called reverse causation. Ask yourself: which one came first, and which one made the other happen?
fact: Causation means the process of one thing making another thing happen.
fact: The cause is why something happens; the effect is what happens.
fact: Causation is a formal word for the link showing that one event was really caused by another.
fact: Two things happening together (correlation) does not always mean one causes the other.
fact: Scientists and historians ask: which came first, and could something else be causing both?
exercise: «The heavy rain caused the river to flood the village.» What is the cause in this sentence? | The river's name | The river flooding the village | The village | *The heavy rain
exercise: What does causation mean? | *The process of one thing making another thing happen | A kind of weather | A list of things in order | A loud sound made by a drum
exercise: Ice cream sales and sunburn both rise in summer. Does ice cream cause sunburn? | *No, hot weather may cause both | Yes, sunburn makes people buy ice cream | Yes, ice cream always causes sunburn | Only in winter
test: «Big fires get more firefighters and also do more damage.» Which way does the cause go? | Firefighters cause the fires | *The fire brings more firefighters | Damage makes fires go quiet | Nothing is connected at all
test: What is reverse causation? | *Mixing up which way the cause goes | Two things that never happen together | A fire that burns backwards | A type of fire engine
exam: «When two things happen together, one does not have to cause the other», so which phrase warns you about this? | *correlation is not causation | Pass the salt | Once upon a time | The sky is blue
